Task 1: Analyzing IdM Authentication Logs
Subtask 1.1: Understanding IdM Log Structure
First, let's explore the IdM logging structure and identify key log files.

Connect to your IdM server and examine the log directory structure:
sudo ls -la /var/log/ipa/
Identify the main log files used for authentication troubleshooting:
sudo ls -la /var/log/ipa/ | grep -E "(install|server|client|krb5kdc)"
Check the current log rotation settings:
sudo cat /etc/logrotate.d/ipa
Subtask 1.2: Monitoring Real-Time Authentication Events
Open multiple terminal sessions to monitor logs in real-time:
# Terminal 1 - Monitor main IPA server log
sudo tail -f /var/log/ipa/ipaserver-install.log

# Terminal 2 - Monitor Kerberos KDC log
sudo tail -f /var/log/krb5kdc.log

# Terminal 3 - Monitor system authentication log
sudo tail -f /var/log/secure
Generate authentication events by attempting to authenticate:
# From another terminal or client machine
kinit testuser@EXAMPLE.COM
Analyze the log entries and identify the authentication flow patterns.
Subtask 1.3: Parsing Authentication Failure Logs
Create a script to extract authentication failures:
sudo nano /usr/local/bin/ipa-auth-failures.sh
Add the following content:

#!/bin/bash
# IPA Authentication Failure Analysis Script

echo "=== IPA Authentication Failures Analysis ==="
echo "Date Range: Last 24 hours"
echo

# Check for authentication failures in secure log
echo "--- Authentication Failures from /var/log/secure ---"
sudo grep -i "authentication failure" /var/log/secure | tail -20

echo
echo "--- Kerberos Authentication Failures ---"
sudo grep -i "FAILED" /var/log/krb5kdc.log | tail -20

echo
echo "--- LDAP Bind Failures ---"
sudo grep -i "bind failed" /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-*/access | tail -10

echo
echo "--- Recent Failed Login Attempts ---"
sudo lastb | head -10
Make the script executable and run it:
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/ipa-auth-failures.sh
sudo /usr/local/bin/ipa-auth-failures.sh
Task 2: Troubleshooting Common IdM Authentication Issues
Subtask 2.1: Diagnosing Password-Related Issues
Check user account status for authentication failures:
# Check if user account is locked
sudo ipa user-show testuser --all

# Check password expiration
sudo ipa pwpolicy-show
Create a test scenario with expired password:
# Set password to expire immediately for testing
sudo ipa user-mod testuser --password-expiration=$(date -d "yesterday" +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)Z

# Attempt authentication
kinit testuser@EXAMPLE.COM
Resolve password expiration issues:
# Reset password expiration
sudo ipa user-mod testuser --password-expiration=""

# Force password change on next login
sudo ipa user-mod testuser --password-expiration=$(date -d "tomorrow" +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)Z
Subtask 2.2: Troubleshooting Account Lockout Issues
Check account lockout policy:
sudo ipa pwpolicy-show --all
Simulate account lockout by multiple failed attempts:
# Create a script to simulate failed logins
for i in {1..6}; do
    echo "wrongpassword" | kinit testuser@EXAMPLE.COM 2>/dev/null
    echo "Attempt $i completed"
    sleep 1
done
Check if account is locked:
sudo ipa user-status testuser
Unlock the account:
sudo ipa user-unlock testuser
Subtask 2.3: Resolving Certificate-Related Authentication Issues
Check IdM server certificates:
# Check certificate validity
sudo ipa-getcert list

# Check certificate expiration
sudo openssl x509 -in /var/lib/ipa/certs/httpd.crt -text -noout | grep -A2 "Validity"
Verify certificate trust:
# Check if CA certificate is properly installed
sudo certutil -L -d /etc/pki/nssdb/

# Verify certificate chain
sudo openssl verify -CAfile /etc/ipa/ca.crt /var/lib/ipa/certs/httpd.crt
Renew certificates if needed:
# Request certificate renewal
sudo ipa-getcert resubmit -i <request_id>

# Check renewal status
sudo ipa-getcert list -i <request_id>
Task 3: Resolving DNS and Kerberos Issues
Subtask 3.1: Diagnosing DNS Configuration Issues
Verify DNS resolution for IdM services:
# Check SRV records for Kerberos
dig _kerberos._tcp.example.com SRV

# Check SRV records for LDAP
dig _ldap._tcp.example.com SRV

# Verify A record for IdM server
dig ipa.example.com A
Create a DNS diagnostic script:
sudo nano /usr/local/bin/ipa-dns-check.sh
Add the following content:

#!/bin/bash
# IPA DNS Configuration Check Script

DOMAIN="example.com"
IPA_SERVER="ipa.example.com"

echo "=== IPA DNS Configuration Check ==="
echo

echo "--- Checking DNS Server Configuration ---"
cat /etc/resolv.conf

echo
echo "--- Checking Kerberos SRV Records ---"
dig _kerberos._tcp.$DOMAIN SRV +short

echo
echo "--- Checking LDAP SRV Records ---"
dig _ldap._tcp.$DOMAIN SRV +short

echo
echo "--- Checking IPA Server A Record ---"
dig $IPA_SERVER A +short

echo
echo "--- Reverse DNS Lookup ---"
IPA_IP=$(dig $IPA_SERVER A +short)
dig -x $IPA_IP +short

echo
echo "--- Testing DNS Resolution Speed ---"
time nslookup $IPA_SERVER >/dev/null
Run the DNS diagnostic script:
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/ipa-dns-check.sh
sudo /usr/local/bin/ipa-dns-check.sh
